## 💻 Quick Start

### Basic Usage

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "time"
    
    "github.com/jamesleeon/shardcache"
)

func main() {
    // Create a cache instance with cleanup every 10 minutes
    c := cache.New(10 * time.Minute)
    
    // Set a key-value pair with 5-minute TTL
    c.Set("my_key", "Hello World!", 5*time.Minute)
    
    // Get the value
    if val, found := c.Get("my_key"); found {
        fmt.Println("Found:", val)
        // Output: Found: Hello World!
    }
}
```

### Expiration Validation

```go
// Wait for expiration
time.Sleep(6 * time.Minute)

// Key has expired, get returns not found
if _, found := c.Get("my_key"); !found {
    fmt.Println("Key expired as expected.")
}
```

### Atomic Operation: GetOrSet

```go
// Scenario 1: Key doesn't exist, compute function is executed
val, wasPresent := c.GetOrSet("key2", func() (interface{}, time.Duration) {
    return "Computed Value", time.Hour
})
fmt.Printf("GetOrSet: %s (wasPresent: %v)\n", val, wasPresent)
// Output: GetOrSet: Computed Value (wasPresent: false)

// Scenario 2: Key exists, returns existing value without executing compute function
val, wasPresent = c.GetOrSet("key2", func() (interface{}, time.Duration) {
    return "New Value", time.Hour  // Will NOT be executed
})
fmt.Printf("GetOrSet: %s (wasPresent: %v)\n", val, wasPresent)
// Output: GetOrSet: Computed Value (wasPresent: true)
```

---

## 🏗️ Core Architecture

### Sharding Technology

By distributing cache data across multiple shards, each with independent locking:
- Reduces lock contention probability
- Improves concurrent access performance
- Achieves near-linear performance scaling

### Object Reuse (sync.Pool)

Utilizes Go's standard library sync.Pool to reuse cache item objects:
- Reduces memory allocation frequency
- Lowers GC pressure
- Achieves 0 allocs/op for overwrite operations

